Hono server. Routes are registered in `src/app.ts`; `src/index.ts` is the entry point. Handlers stay thin; shared logic goes in modules of its own, under `src`.

{{#if (has "deployment" "cloudflare")}}
The app is a Worker: `dev` runs `wrangler dev` in the Workers runtime, so the bindings declared in `wrangler.jsonc` are on `c.env`. It listens on port {{appPort appName}}. The `PORT` environment variable overrides it, which is how portless reaches it. There is no `start` script and no `src/server.ts`: nothing serves the app from Node or Bun.
{{else}}
{{#if (eq pm "bun")}}`dev` and `start` run under Bun{{else}}`dev` and `start` run on Node through `tsx`, from `src/server.ts` (it serves the app of `src/index.ts` with `@hono/node-server`){{/if}} and listen on port {{appPort appName}}. The `PORT` environment variable overrides it.
{{/if}}

The error handler in `src/app.ts` keeps the status and message of an `HTTPException` and answers any other error with a generic 500; the real error is always logged with its stack. It returns the message and stack only when `NODE_ENV=development`{{#if (has "deployment" "cloudflare")}}, which is the case under `{{runScript "dev"}}` and `{{runAppScript "preview"}}` (Wrangler builds the Worker for development) and not once deployed{{else}}, so run `NODE_ENV=development {{runScript "dev"}}` to see them in responses{{/if}}. Throw `HTTPException` for errors meant for the client.
